  "about": "The evangelium of the formulary “16th Sunday after Pentecost” in the 1962 Roman Missal.",
  "segments": {
    "s01": { "translation": "At that time, as Jesus was going into the house of a certain leader of the Pharisees to eat bread on the Sabbath, they were watching Him closely. And behold, there was a certain man before Him who had dropsy. And Jesus, answering, spoke to the experts in the law and the Pharisees, saying: “Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?” But they were silent. And He took hold of him, healed him, and sent him away. And answering them, He said: “If the donkey or the ox of any of you falls into a well, will he not pull it out at once on the Sabbath day?” And they could give Him no answer to this. And He also told a parable to those who had been invited, noticing how they picked out the chief places at table, and said to them: “When you are invited to a wedding, do not sit down in the first place, lest perhaps someone more honorable than you may have been invited by the host, and he who invited both you and him may come and say to you: ‘Give this man your place,’ and then you may begin with shame to take the last place. But when you are invited, go and sit down in the last place, so that when he who invited you comes, he may say to you: ‘Friend, go up higher.’ Then you will have glory in the presence of those who sit at table with you. For everyone who exalts himself will be humbled, and he who humbles himself will be exalted.”" }
  },
